a-na-kam sa ki-ma Su Ku bi 4 im kà ra am im-hu-ru-ma um-ma su-nu-ma A-sùr-na-da a-na ni-kà-si u-la i-za-az Su-Ku-bu-um lu ra-bi-su-nu-ma a-si-a-tI ni-it-ta-la-ma a-na ni-kà-sI a-na gam-ra-tim-ma nu-us-ta-zi-iz-kà 6 TUG ku-ta-ni 6 TUG su-ru-tim a-na É kà-ri-im ta-di 1/2 ma-na.TA a-na TUG ku-ta-ni 18 GiN.TA a-na su-ru-tim a-na ni-kà-sI ta-sa-kkn i-na a-wa-at A-lim sa a-na ni-ka-si i-sa-hu-tù-ni hu-bu-lam sa kà-ri-im KÙ.B i-sa-qal,Sii-Kubum's representatives applied to the colony here and said: Assur-nada will not stand for the settlement of accounts. Let Sn-Kubum be their boss. Because of this we deliberated and (decided to) make you stand for the entire settlement. You have deposited 6 kutanum-textiles and 6 surum-textiles at the office of the colony. You may charge (the account) for the settlement with I/2 mina for each kutanum and 18 shekels per surum (i.e. 4 minas 48 shekels of silver). Whoever withdraws for the settlement will pay the debt of the colony in silver in accordance with the rule of the City.,"Dercksen, J. G. - Old Assyrian Institutions, MOS 4. PIHANS 98, 2004 (2).pdf",116
